# The `permissions` API # {#permissions-api}

The <dfn id="permissions-api-concept">permissions</dfn> API lets an extension check, request, and relinquish its own
[=optional permissions=] and [=optional host permissions=] at runtime, instead of requiring
every permission at install time.

Issue: [=Optional permissions=] and [=optional host permissions=] are not yet defined (see the
empty [`optional_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_permissions) and
[`optional_host_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_host_permissions) sections). Until defined, take them to mean: a
permission or host pattern that is either listed in `optional_permissions`/`optional_host_permissions`,
or listed in `permissions`/`host_permissions` and withheld from the extension until requested.

<pre class="idl">
dictionary Permissions {
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; permissions;
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; origins;
};

[Exposed=WebExtensions]
interface permissions {
  Promise&lt;Permissions&gt; getAll();
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; contains(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; request(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; remove(optional Permissions permissions);

  attribute EventHandler onAdded;
  attribute EventHandler onRemoved;
};
</pre>

Issue: All three engines additionally accept a callback function as a trailing argument on
every method here, resolved/invoked instead of returning a promise when supplied. This
specification defines only the promise form here; see [Promises and callbacks](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#promises-and-callbacks) (currently empty)
for how dual promise/callback signatures are expressed generally.

Issue: Chromium's `permissions.json` also declares `addHostAccessRequest` and
`removeHostAccessRequest`, and Firefox declares a separate `AnyPermissions` dictionary (adding a
`data_collection` member, pref-gated, used only by `getAll` and `contains`) distinct from the
`Permissions` dictionary used by `request`/`remove`/the events. Both are Chromium- or
Firefox-specific; whether either belongs in the shared spec is undecided.

## The `Permissions` dictionary ## {#permissions-dictionary}

A {{Permissions}} dictionary's `permissions` member is a list of named API permission strings,
excluding host permissions. Its `origins` member is a list of [=match pattern=]s.

Issue: All three engines validate `permissions` strings against a closed, engine-specific set of
recognized permission names, and validate `origins` entries using the same [=match pattern=]
grammar used for the `host_permissions` manifest key. What the shared set of recognized
permission names is, and how strictly it is enforced, is not yet specified normatively.

Issue: Firefox additionally recognizes a `data_collection` member on the dictionary it accepts
and returns, listing data-collection permission strings declared in the manifest's
`data_collection_permissions` key. Neither Chromium nor WebKit implement a `data_collection`
concept. Whether data-collection permissions are in scope for this specification, and if so
whether {{Permissions}} should include a `data_collection` member, is unresolved.

## `getAll()` ## {#permissions-getall}

<dfn method for="permissions">getAll()</dfn> returns the extension's complete current set of
permissions and [=match pattern=]s: both the permissions and host permissions required by the
manifest, and any optional or runtime-host permissions currently granted.

1. Let |result| be a new {{Permissions}} dictionary.
1. Set |result|["permissions"] to the extension's currently active named API permissions,
    combining permissions required by the manifest and optional permissions currently granted.
1. Set |result|["origins"] to the extension's currently active host [=match pattern=]s,
    combining host permissions required by the manifest and optional or runtime host
    permissions currently granted.
1. Return |result|.

Issue: Whether the returned `origins` list may include a synthesized "matches all hosts"
pattern that is not literally present in the manifest or in any single granted permission, when
the union of currently granted host access happens to cover all hosts, is engine-specific and
unresolved. One implementation does this; the other two return only the origins actually
declared or granted.

## `contains()` ## {#permissions-contains}

<dfn method for="permissions">contains(permissions)</dfn> checks whether the extension currently
has every permission and origin named in |permissions|.

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. For each named permission in |permissions|["permissions"]: if it is not currently active on
    the extension, return `false`.
1. For each [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"]: if it is not subsumed by a currently
    active host [=match pattern=] on the extension, return `false`.
1. Return `true`.

Issue: what happens when a `permissions` or `origins` entry is syntactically well-formed but not
a permission name or pattern the engine recognizes at all is engine-specific: one implementation
rejects the returned promise, one throws synchronously before the returned promise is even
created, and the third throws synchronously for this method specifically while resolving or
rejecting a promise for the same condition in `request()`/`remove()`.

## `request()` ## {#permissions-request}

<dfn method for="permissions">request(permissions)</dfn> asks the user to grant the extension
some or all of |permissions|, where those are not already active. It must only be called while
handling a [=user gesture=].

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. If this call is not happening within a [=user gesture=], reject with an error.
1. If any named permission in |permissions|["permissions"] is not declared in the extension's
    `optional_permissions` manifest key, reject with an error.
1. If any [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"] is not subsumed by a [=match pattern=]
    declared in the extension's `optional_host_permissions` manifest key, or by a
    `host_permissions` entry withheld from the extension pending user approval, reject with an
    error.
1. If every named permission and [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is already active on the
    extension, resolve with `true`.
1. Prompt the user to approve the permissions and origins in |permissions| that are not already
    active. User agents may skip the prompt, and grant automatically, when the newly requested
    permissions and origins carry no additional user-facing warnings beyond what the extension
    already has active.
1. If the user does not approve the prompt, resolve with `false`.
1. Grant the extension the requested permissions and origins that were not already active. This
    fires [=permissions/onAdded=] for the newly granted set.
1. Resolve with `true`.

Issue: The three implementations diverge on what happens when there is no window available to
show the permission prompt in, independently of the user-gesture requirement above. One
implementation treats "no active window" as its own rejection distinct from "no user gesture".
Another has no concept of an active window at this layer at all, delegates prompting to the
embedding application, and silently resolves `false` if the application never responds within a
fixed timeout. The third has no window requirement either and dispatches the prompt as a
notification carrying whatever UI surface is available. This specification defines only the
outcomes all three agree on (reject with no gesture; resolve `false` if the application-level
prompt is never approved) and leaves the window requirement itself unspecified.

Issue: Whether an extension may call `request()` from a context other than its background or
extension pages, specifically from a content script, is a genuine three-way split. Two
implementations exclude the entire `permissions` namespace from content scripts. The third
exposes `request()` specifically to content scripts while keeping the rest of the namespace
restricted to background/extension-page contexts. Which behavior is normative is unresolved.

Issue: Whether a managed/enterprise policy may cause `request()` to reject outright, before any
prompt, is implemented in two of the three engines; the third engine's behavior here is
unconfirmed.

## `remove()` ## {#permissions-remove}

<dfn method for="permissions">remove(permissions)</dfn> relinquishes some or all of the
extension's currently active optional permissions and origins.

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is required by the extension's
    manifest (not optional, and not a withheld host permission the extension chose to give up
    separately), reject with an error.
1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is not declared anywhere in the
    extension's manifest, reject with an error.
1. Revoke each named permission and [=match pattern=] in |permissions| that is currently active
    on the extension. Entries in |permissions| that are not currently active are ignored rather
    than treated as an error. This fires [=permissions/onRemoved=] for the revoked set, if any.
1. Resolve with `true`.

## `onAdded` ## {#permissions-onadded}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onAdded</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins newly granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's active permissions increase. This includes grants made through
[=permissions/request=], and grants made through user agent UI outside of the `permissions` API
(for example, a browser action letting the user grant a withheld host permission).

Issue: whether a permission or host-permission grant driven entirely by administrative/managed
policy also fires `onAdded` is a confirmed divergence for at least one engine: that engine
explicitly does not fire `onAdded`/`onRemoved` for policy-driven changes. Whether the other two
engines' policy-driven grants go through the same code path as user- and API-driven grants, and
therefore also fire the event, is unconfirmed.

## `onRemoved` ## {#permissions-onremoved}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onRemoved</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins no longer granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's active permissions decrease, whether through [=permissions/remove=] or through user
agent UI.
